# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment template. Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values
# for local development:   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# In production (Render/Railway) do NOT upload any .env file — set these as
# environment variables in the host dashboard instead. Real secrets must never
# be committed to git.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------- Payments ----------------
# Payment mode: transfer | mock
#   transfer -> the real thing (default). Buyers pay the SELLER directly: by
#               bank transfer / PayPal.Me (seller confirms by hand), or
#               instantly through the seller's OWN PayPal or Stripe account,
#               which each seller connects under Portal -> Settings. The
#               platform never holds money and has no payment credentials.
#   mock     -> fake test payments (local only; blocked in production unless
#               ALLOW_MOCK_PAYMENTS=true).
PAYMENT_MODE=transfer

# PAYPAL_ENV / PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID / PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET are NO LONGER USED —
# there is no platform PayPal account. Leave them unset. (PAYPAL_ENV=live is
# still honoured as a "this is the live site" signal that blocks mock mode.)

# Only for testing a production build WITHOUT real payments. Keep false/unset live.
ALLOW_MOCK_PAYMENTS=false

# ---------------- App ----------------
# Public URL of the site (used in ticket links inside emails).
NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://nepalimarkt.com

# Google Analytics 4 measurement ID. LEAVE IT EMPTY and no analytics runs at
# all — no script, no cookie, nothing loaded. Set it and the tag goes on the
# PUBLIC pages only: never on /order, /ticket, /c, /scan, /admin or /portal,
# because those URLs carry a credential in them and GA reports the URL. Query
# strings are never sent either, on any page.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ means this is baked in at BUILD time, so it has to be set in
# your host's environment (Render → Environment) and the app rebuilt — setting
# it on a running server changes nothing.
#
# In Germany it may only run after consent (§ 25 TDDDG + Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR).
# The app asks first (components/ConsentBanner) and loads nothing until the
# visitor says yes; /privacy describes Google Analytics only while this is set.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_ID=

# Long random string that signs admin sessions. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
APP_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# Password for the /admin area. In production it must be >= 10 characters and
# not a common placeholder.
ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-strong-password

# ---------------- Email (optional but recommended) ----------------
# Sends ticket + payment-instruction emails and admin sign-in codes.
#
# SendGrid (recommended): SMTP_USER is the literal word "apikey"; SMTP_PASS is
# an API key ("SG.....") from https://app.sendgrid.com/settings/api_keys with
# Mail Send permission. The EMAIL_FROM address must be verified in SendGrid
# under Settings -> Sender Authentication (Single Sender or verified domain).
#
# Gmail alternative: SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com, SMTP_USER=you@gmail.com, and
# SMTP_PASS is a 16-char App Password from
# https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords.
SMTP_HOST=smtp.sendgrid.net
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=apikey
SMTP_PASS=
# The one address everything is sent from, unless split below. It must be a
# verified sender at the mail provider. Only the ADDRESS is used: the name the
# inbox shows is set per mail by the code (lib/platform senderFor) —
# "MartNepali Tickets", "MartNepali Payments", "MartNepali Support", … — so a
# name written here is ignored.
EMAIL_FROM="MartNepali <support@martnepali.com>"

# Two audiences, two addresses. Both optional — each falls back to EMAIL_FROM.
# Both must be VERIFIED SENDERS in SendGrid (Settings > Sender Authentication),
# or SendGrid rejects the send outright.
#
#   EMAIL_FROM_BUYERS      tickets, payment instructions, reminders, answered
#                          questions — anything to somebody who bought a ticket
#   EMAIL_FROM_ORGANIZERS  account credentials, sign-in codes, sign-in alerts,
#                          fee instructions, payout-change alarms
#
# Where REPLIES should go, when that is not the same as the From.
#
# Sending and receiving are separate services and they arrive at different times:
# a domain can be authenticated for sending within minutes while having no
# mailbox at all. In that window a Reply-To at your own domain BOUNCES every
# reply a buyer sends, so point these at an inbox that exists — a personal
# address is fine — and delete them once real mailboxes are in place.
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO_BUYERS=you@gmail.com
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO_ORGANIZERS=you@gmail.com

# Reply-To is otherwise set explicitly on every message to the same address as its From,
# so a reply lands on the desk that sent it rather than wherever a mail client
# guesses. The exceptions are deliberate: a forwarded contact request replies to
# the person who wrote in, not to us.
#
# Worth splitting for triage — a buyer asking "where do I collect?" and an
# organizer whose payout account just moved need different people — and because
# the second is a security channel. An alarm inbox that also takes every buyer
# question is an inbox whose alarms get skimmed.
# BOTH ARE OPTIONAL, and one address for everything is a perfectly good place to
# start — leave these unset and every message uses EMAIL_FROM. Only split them
# when you have a second mailbox that somebody actually reads.
#
# Whatever you use must be at a domain you control, and either domain-
# authenticated or a verified sender in SendGrid. The address inside the angle
# brackets is the only part that counts: "Events <you@gmail.com>" reads
# perfectly and is still refused, because authenticating your domain does
# nothing for an address that is not at it.
#
# Do not send from an address that cannot RECEIVE. Every reply to it bounces —
# and a buyer's reply to their ticket email is the one message you most want.
# EMAIL_FROM_BUYERS="MartNepali <tickets@eventsticketbuy.com>"
# EMAIL_FROM_ORGANIZERS="MartNepali Support <support@eventsticketbuy.com>"

# Admin two-step login: sign-in codes are emailed here (comma-separate for
# several admins).
ADMIN_EMAIL=you@gmail.com

# ---------------- Admin login code by SMS (optional) ----------------
# Also text the 6-digit admin sign-in code to your phone (via Twilio). Leave
# blank for email-only login.
ADMIN_PHONE=
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
TWILIO_FROM=

# Set to "off" to disable two-step login and use password only (not recommended).
#ADMIN_OTP=off

# TEMPORARY shared sign-in code for testers (optional). While set, this exact
# code (minimum 8 characters, or it is ignored) is accepted in place of the
# emailed 6-digit code — the admin password is still required first. Remove it
# to revoke access instantly. Do NOT leave this set during real ticket sales.
ADMIN_MASTER_OTP=

# ---------------- Hidden admin URL (optional) ----------------
# When set, /admin returns 404 for everyone; unlock it on your device by
# visiting https://your-domain.com/<this-value> once. Keep it secret like a
# password. Leave blank to use a plain /admin.
ADMIN_GATE_PATH=

# ---------------- Storage ----------------
# Where the SQLite database lives. On Render, point this at your mounted disk.
DATA_DIR=./data

# Days after an event's date before its buyers' names and email addresses are
# irreversibly overwritten in the database (the orders, tickets, revenue and
# check-in history all stay — only the person stops being identifiable).
# Default 30. The /privacy page and the checkout notice both print THIS number,
# so changing it changes what buyers are promised. Events with no date set are
# never swept: there is nothing to count the window from.
BUYER_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# ---------------- Platform (organizer-facing business settings) ----------------
# Public platform name (header, page titles, emails).
PLATFORM_NAME=MartNepali

# There is no platform fee any more — the fee machinery was deliberately
# deleted (see src/lib/platform.ts). The platform is free while it grows, so
# there are no PLATFORM_FEE_* or PLATFORM_BANK_* variables to set: the only
# bank accounts in the system are the organizers' own payout details.

# The public support address (/contact, Impressum, privacy policy, AVV, and
# the inbox contact-form enquiries go to) is NOT a setting: it is SUPPORT_EMAIL
# in src/lib/platform.ts, support@martnepali.com. PLATFORM_CONTACT_EMAIL used
# to override it, and a stale value on the host kept a dead address on every
# legal page — so it is no longer read. Change the address in code.

# ---------- AI assistant (visitor Q&A on event pages) — optional ----------
# Free key from Google AI Studio: https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# Without it the assistant still works, but every question is passed to the
# organizer to answer by hand instead of being answered instantly.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Leave UNSET. The code uses the floating alias "gemini-flash-latest" so that
# Google retiring a model version cannot silently kill the assistant — which is
# exactly what happened when this app pinned gemini-2.0-flash.
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-flash-latest

# ---- Gemini and personal data (read before setting any of these) ----
# Content that can contain personal data — visitor questions, voice
# recordings, the contact chat, the organizer assistants — goes to Gemini ONLY
# under Google's PAID terms (not used to improve Google's products; Google's
# data processing addendum applies). On the unpaid tier Google may use what it
# is sent, so without one of the two settings below those features fall back
# to Anthropic (if ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is set) or to their non-AI behaviour.
# /privacy and the AVV promise exactly this — see src/lib/legal.ts.
#
# Option A (cheap): a key from a SEPARATE Google Cloud project with billing on.
# Only personal-content calls use it; page translation (published page text
# only, nothing about buyers) keeps running free on GEMINI_API_KEY.
GEMINI_PAID_API_KEY=
#
# Daily ceiling on PAID Gemini calls across the whole site, so the bill cannot
# run away. Past it, the features fall back as if no paid key were set.
# Default 100.
# GEMINI_PAID_DAILY_CAP=100
#
# Option B: billing is on for GEMINI_API_KEY's own project, so every call is
# paid anyway — then set this to 1 instead.
GEMINI_PAID_TIER=
#
# NOTE: Google's Gemini API terms allow only PAID services for apps offered to
# users in the EEA, Switzerland or the UK — consider putting translation on a
# billed key as well.

# Speech-to-text model for the mic buttons. Uses GEMINI_API_KEY. Left unset it
# uses gemini-flash-lite-latest, which is deliberate: the premium flash model
# allows only 20 requests PER DAY on the free tier and shares its allowance with
# the visitor assistant, so dictation on it would take the assistant down too.
GEMINI_TRANSCRIBE_MODEL=

# Emailed 6-digit code at ORGANIZER sign-in, for accounts with no authenticator.
# Set to "on" ONLY once the SendGrid sending domain is authenticated and you have
# confirmed a code actually reaches a Gmail address. Until then it is off on
# purpose: SendGrid accepts mail that is later dropped silently, so the send
# looks successful while the organizer receives nothing — and every organizer
# without an authenticator would be locked out at once, with nobody able to sign
# in and switch it back off. An authenticator needs no network and is unaffected.
# PORTAL_EMAIL_OTP=on

# Organizers must have an authenticator app before they can PUBLISH an event —
# the moment an account stops being a draft and starts taking strangers' money.
# On by default. Set to "off" only to unblock an organizer who genuinely cannot
# run one; note that their payout changes then fall back to the account
# password, which is the weakest of the checks and the one somebody who phished
# the password can answer.
# PORTAL_REQUIRE_AUTHENTICATOR=off

# ---------------- Example bazaar ----------------
# On a site that has never had them, the clearly marked example shops
# (scripts/seed-bazaar.mjs) are put up ONCE automatically, in the background,
# the first time the homepage or /market is opened. Take them down any time in
# /admin → Example bazaar (they then stay down). Set to "off" to never do it.
# EXAMPLE_BAZAAR_AUTOSEED=off

# --- Customer accounts: "Continue with Google" (optional) ---------------------
# Customers can always sign in with a code sent to their email. To add the
# Google button, create an OAuth client (type "Web application") in Google
# Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials, add the authorised redirect URI
#   https://www.martnepali.com/api/account/google/callback
# and set both values. The button stays hidden until both are set.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------- Legal pages (Impressum, Datenschutz, AVV) ----------------
# Who runs this site, as printed on /impressum (§ 5 DDG), /privacy (the
# controller, Art. 13 GDPR), /terms and in the Auftragsverarbeitungsvertrag
# (/avv). Every one has a built-in default in src/lib/legal.ts, so a legal page
# never renders empty — set these only to CHANGE what is printed.
#
# Name of the natural person operating the platform.
# OPERATOR_NAME=Milan Adhikari
#
# Ladungsfähige Anschrift (a real postal address, not a P.O. box). Separate the
# lines with ";" (or a literal \n).
# OPERATOR_ADDRESS=Donnersbergring 26;64295 Darmstadt;Deutschland
#
# The Impressum / privacy email is SUPPORT_EMAIL in src/lib/platform.ts (see
# above) — § 5 DDG requires one that is actually read. The pages always offer
# the /contact form beside it as the second fast channel.
#
# Optional telephone number for the Impressum (email + contact form suffice).
# OPERATOR_PHONE=
#
# Where Render runs this service, printed on /privacy and in AVV Anlage 1.
# Default: "Frankfurt am Main, Deutschland (EU)" (the confirmed region). If you
# ever move the service to another Render region, set this in the same breath —
# a region outside the EU also changes the privacy text (a third-country
# transfer must be disclosed).
# HOSTING_REGION=Frankfurt am Main, Deutschland (EU)
